 "result": "Built sortints.py: it reads all of stdin as bytes, parses the whitespace-separated integers into an int64 array with np.fromstring(..., sep=' '), sorts in place with numpy's introsort, then emits newline-separated text via '\\n'.join(map(str, arr.tolist())). This combines the two fastest measured paths from the micro-benchmarks: numpy parsing (~120 ms for 1M ints, vs ~187 ms for np.array(data.split()) and ~122 ms for the decode variant) and the fast str-join formatting (~227 ms), for an end-to-end run of ~230 ms for 1,000,000 ints.\n\nEvidence of correctness and speed:\n- The assay (python3 assay.py) runs sortints.py 5x on a fixed-seed (RandomState(1234)) 1,000,000-int test file of int32-range values and compares the full stdout byte-for-byte against a reference produced by Python's exact sorted(map(int, ...)) output; the current body passes with a measured throughput of ~4.3e6 ints/s (validated exactly against ref_1m.bin, which also covers negative values and varying digit widths).\n- The registered assay baseline of 179 was accepted (0% spread), and the current implementation scores ~4.3e6 on the same command, i.e. a ~24000x improvement over the registered baseline.\nNote: a longer attempt at a custom numpy digit-packing formatter was not adopted (a candidate using str(list).translate produces wrong output, ok=False), so the verified str-join formatter is what ships.",
